ANONYMOUS PRIVACY:

No public profile should display anonymous posts.

Anonymous posts must not appear in:

- Member profile timelines.
- Public member activity.
- Public member post history.
- Public profile search results.

Only Supreme Admin can reveal the real account behind an anonymous contribution.

Moderators cannot reveal anonymous identities.

IMPORTANT BACKEND ARCHITECTURE:

Never put the real author's UID or private account identity in a publicly readable anonymous post document.

Separate public anonymous content from private identity mappings.

The public anonymous feed must not expose:

- Author UID.
- Author email.
- Author phone number.
- Real name.
- Private profile link.
- Any direct identifier.

Private anonymous identity mappings must only be accessible through a secure server-side operation that verifies Supreme Admin authority.

Moderator operations must never return private anonymous identity information.

Keep private audit records when Supreme Admin reveals an identity.

Do not expose those audit records to ordinary members or Moderators.

ANONYMOUS FEED:

- Newest posts first.
- Use reliable server timestamps.
- Avoid duplicate posts when refreshing.
- Support comments and nested replies.
- Support likes and reactions.
- Support search and categories.
- Support private saved posts.
- Support shareable post links.

Deleting a post must eventually delete its nested replies and associated interaction records.

Do not pretend Firestore automatically cascades deletes. Design a secure server-side deletion operation for later implementation.

PERMISSION CONFLICT RULE:

If a Moderator is prohibited from deleting content belonging to Verified or Moderator users, that protection also applies to their anonymous contributions.

The backend must enforce this even though the Moderator cannot see the anonymous author's identity.

